  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nHigh pressure over the lower Ohio Valley will start to build\ninto the area today. Most of the cloud cover has eroded away\nacross northwest and north central Ohio. Lingering lake effect\nclouds are still holding on to far NEOH and NWPA this morning.\nHigh temperatures will be seasonable today in the middle to\nupper 50s. A weak cold front will brush by to the northeast\ntonight and bring a few scattered rain showers to far NEOH and\nNWPA. Monday's high temperatures will be similar as today in the\nlower to middle 50s. High pressure will remain in control of the\nweather pattern through mid week with high temperatures in the\nmiddle 50s to lower 60s through Wednesday. Dry weather is\nexpected Monday through Wednesday afternoon. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nModel guidance indicate the potential for an unsettled and\ncolder weather pattern by the end of this week. There are some\nforecast model differences and uncertainty Thursday through\nSaturday. The main message for the long term forecast period\nwill be an deep upper level trough will develop and dig across\nthe Great Lakes and much of the eastern U.S. Thursday through\nSaturday. A low pressure system and cold front will sweep\nthrough the Ohio Valley into the New England region Thursday\ninto Friday. Depending on the track and strength of this system\nwill have an impact on what type of weather we will see. Right\nnow, we will mention colder temperatures and the likely chance\nfor rain and possible mixed with some wet snow at times \nThursday through Saturday. High temps will be in the 40s and\novernight temps in the 30s.",
